Unix utility 'clock'

The Unix utility 'clock' displays the current time in the top right corner of the console,
updating it once every second.

This utility never finishes on its own. If it was started directly, without running in the
background, it can be terminated with the F1 key. It can also be started in the background using
the sh, hash or ash shells, appending an ampersand character to the command, for example
'clock &', and later terminated with the 'kill' utility, informing the process identification
number shown when it was started (or using the PID shown in 'ps' or 'top' utilities).

USAGE:

clock

No additional parameters are required.

For more information, enter 'clock ?' or 'clock --help'.

HISTORY

This command appeared in the Hexagonix Dormin development branch (2026).

